<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>com.techcombank.qe</groupId>
    <artifactId>qe-harness-parent</artifactId>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>
  <artifactId>qe-harness-gatling-karate</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <!--
    TST-030's module: the first non-JMeter harness toolchain (Task 20). Proves
    TST-012's headline claim, that one .feature file drives both a Karate
    functional run and a Gatling performance run, so unlike the jmeter
    module there is no forked engine JVM/subprocess indirection: Karate
    itself is a plain JUnit 5 library (Tst030ContractRunner runs
    com.intuit.karate.Runner directly, in-process) and karate-gatling wires
    the SAME classpath feature file into a Gatling Simulation
    (Tst030Simulation.scala) via karateFeature(...). See tst-030-contract/
    README.md for the full mechanism.
  -->
  <properties>
    <!-- Overridable via -D for the Gatling simulation run
         (com.techcombank.qe.harness.gatlingkarate.Tst030Simulation), matching
         SUT_BASE_URL's own convention every other harness module uses (see
         ModuleRunner.java): Gatling simulations read System.getProperty,
         not the environment, when launched via gatling-maven-plugin's own
         forked JVM. -->
    <qe.gatling.sutBaseUrl>http://localhost:8080</qe.gatling.sutBaseUrl>

    <!-- Karate 1.4.1's com.intuit.karate.Suite#run (invoked by
         Runner.Builder#parallel, which Tst030ContractRunner calls directly)
         hangs indefinitely, with zero CPU and no exception, when the
         Surefire-forked test JVM itself runs on JDK 25 (confirmed
         empirically: identical test code, only the forking JVM changed,
         reproduces/fixes this deterministically; jstack showed the main
         thread permanently parked in CompletableFuture.join() with no
         Karate worker thread ever scheduled). This is the exact same shape
         of problem run-jmeter.sh already documents for JMeter's own bundled
         Groovy vs. too-new a JDK, just surfacing as a silent hang here
         rather than a loud "Unsupported class file major version" error.
         JDK 21 (confirmed working) is the newest verified-good JVM for this
         module's forked tests. The primary fix is simply launching Maven
         itself under JDK <= 21 (see qe-harness/README.md); the
         gatling-karate-jvm profile below (activated only when
         qe.gatlingKarate.javaRuntime is actually set via -D) is a secondary
         escape hatch for when that is not possible; Surefire's own <jvm>
         element rejects a bare command name like "java" (it must be an
         actual resolvable path), so this property must never be given a
         default value here; that is also why it is wired through a
         conditionally-activated profile instead of a plain <jvm> in this
         module's own main build/plugins block. -->
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- JUnit 5: Tst030ContractRunner's own two proof tests, and the
         plain Karate JUnit runner test itself. Same version as the jmeter
         module's pom, kept consistent across the reactor. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <version>5.9.2</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Karate's own JUnit 5 integration: com.intuit.karate.Runner,
         com.intuit.karate.core.Results. See qe-harness/README.md's Pinned
         Versions table for how this version was resolved. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
      <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
      <version>1.4.1</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- karate-gatling: the com.intuit.karate.gatling.javaapi.KarateDsl
         (karateFeature(...)) Tst030Simulation.scala uses to drive the
         SAME .feature file as the Karate run above. Its own published POM
         (com.intuit.karate:karate-gatling:1.4.1) declares a transitive
         io.gatling.highcharts:gatling-charts-highcharts:3.9.5, unmodified
         below: karate-gatling 1.4.1 (last published ~2023, no newer release
         exists on Maven Central as of this check) is NOT just
         source/DSL-compatible with the newer 3.15.1 engine Task 1 originally
         pinned in qe-harness/README.md; it is genuinely BINARY-INCOMPATIBLE
         with it. Confirmed empirically two different ways against a real
         `mvn -pl gatling-karate io.gatling:gatling-maven-plugin:4.21.11:test`
         invocation: (1) with 3.15.1, after first fixing a real but separate
         netty-version-mediation issue (karate-junit5's transitive
         com.linecorp.armeria:armeria pulls io.netty:netty-transport
         :4.1.96.Final, which nearest-wins over Gatling's own 4.2.14.Final,
         fixed by importing io.netty:netty-bom:4.2.14.Final, no longer needed
         once the engine itself was downgraded, see below), the run still
         crashes with `NoSuchMethodError:
         ProtocolComponentsRegistry.components(ProtocolKey)` inside
         com.intuit.karate.gatling.KarateFeatureActionBuilder.build; that
         internal Gatling method's erased return type changed between the
         two engine releases, and karate-gatling 1.4.1's compiled bytecode
         still calls the old erasure; (2) with 3.9.5 (karate-gatling's own
         originally-tested pairing, kept below), the identical simulation
         runs a real load test against the reference SUT end-to-end,
         including correctly reporting KO on the v2 scenario with
         schema-drift active while v1 stays OK; see
         qe-harness/README.md's "Known Issues" section for the full
         writeup, including why 3.15.1 (the version the rest of the reactor
         would otherwise prefer) is knowingly NOT used here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
      <artifactId>karate-gatling</artifactId>
      <version>1.4.1</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- The actual Gatling engine. Deliberately left at karate-gatling
         1.4.1's own transitive default (3.9.5) rather than bumped to the
         newer 3.15.1; see the exclusion-free karate-gatling dependency's
         own comment just above for the empirical proof of why. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.gatling.highcharts</groupId>
      <artifactId>gatling-charts-highcharts</artifactId>
      <version>3.9.5</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Scala runtime: 2.13.18 rather than gatling-charts-highcharts 3.9.5's
         own pinned 2.13.10 (or karate-gatling 1.4.1's own 2.13.9); Scala
         2.13.x maintains binary compatibility across its whole line, and
         2.13.18 is what this module's scala-maven-plugin zinc compiler
         bridge is already built for in this environment; 2.13.10 hits an
         unrelated zinc/scala-maven-plugin compiler-bridge bootstrap
         failure ("bad constant pool index") the first time it needs to
         compile ITS OWN bridge for that exact version, confirmed reproducible
         and specific to 2.13.10 in this environment. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.scala-lang</groupId>
      <artifactId>scala-library</artifactId>
      <version>2.13.18</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Evidence contract (RunFragment) and EvidenceEmitter (Task 2): both
         Tst030ContractRunner (Karate/JUnit) and Tst030Simulation (Gatling)
         emit a fragment through this same JVM class. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.techcombank.qe</groupId>
      <artifactId>qe-harness-common</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- PostgreSQL JDBC driver: Tst030ContractRunner seeds the two fixture
         accounts (ACC-000001/ACC-000002) this feature's requests transfer
         between, via a direct java.sql.DriverManager query, same pattern
         (and same driver version) as the jmeter module's
         assert-trial-balance.groovy, because neither /v1 nor /v2/transfers
         exposes an account-creation endpoint of its own. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <version>42.7.13</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.0.0-M9</version>
        <configuration>
          <!-- Tst030ContractRunner.java is this module's own required
               deliverable filename (the brief's Files list), which does not
               match Surefire's default *Test/*Tests/*TestCase inclusion
               patterns; without this, `mvn test` silently runs zero
               tests. -->
          <includes>
            <include>**/Tst030ContractRunner.java</include>
          </includes>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Registered (not lifecycle-bound, same "no execution" choice
           harness/jmeter/pom.xml makes for jmeter-maven-plugin, and for the
           identical reason: a plain `mvn test` must never also fire a real
           Gatling load run against whatever SUT happens to be reachable)
           so the load run CAN be invoked directly:

             mvn -pl gatling-karate io.gatling:gatling-maven-plugin:4.21.11:test \
                 "-Dgatling.simulationClass=com.techcombank.qe.harness.gatlingkarate.Tst030Simulation"

           disableCompiler is true because scala-maven-plugin (below) already
           compiles Tst030Simulation.scala into target/test-classes as part of
           this module's normal test-compile phase, the same
           disableCompiler=true choice karate-gatling 1.4.1's own reference
           POM makes, for the same reason (avoid compiling the same sources
           twice with two different Scala compiler invocations). (4.21.11
           itself does not declare this parameter; it logs one harmless
           WARNING and ignores it, but it is kept for forward
           compatibility and because it is the documented, correct setting
           for whichever gatling-maven-plugin version is actually resolved.)

           Verified actually working end-to-end with the command above
           (JDK 21, this module's pinned 3.9.5 engine; see the
           gatling-charts-highcharts dependency's own long comment for why
           3.9.5, not the newer 3.15.1, is pinned): both scenarios run
           against the real reference SUT (POST /v1 and /v2/transfers,
           OK=2 KO=0), and with `schema-drift` active the v2 scenario
           correctly reports KO while v1 stays OK, the same defect-proof
           TST-030's Karate side demonstrates, now demonstrated on the
           Gatling side too. See qe-harness/README.md's "Known Issues"
           section for the full incompatibility writeup that made pinning
           to 3.9.5 necessary. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>io.gatling</groupId>
        <artifactId>gatling-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>4.21.11</version>
        <configuration>
          <disableCompiler>true</disableCompiler>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>

      <!-- Compiles src/test/scala (Tst030Simulation) using its own default
           source-directory convention, alongside plain maven-compiler-plugin
           compiling src/test/java (Tst030ContractRunner) via the default
           lifecycle binding; same mixed-source pattern karate-gatling 1.4.1's
           own build uses (see that artifact's published POM), just with both
           source roots present here instead of only one. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>net.alchim31.maven</groupId>
        <artifactId>scala-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>4.8.1</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>scala-test-compile</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>testCompile</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
        <configuration>
          <args>
            <arg>-release:21</arg>
            <arg>-deprecation</arg>
            <arg>-feature</arg>
            <arg>-language:implicitConversions</arg>
            <arg>-language:postfixOps</arg>
          </args>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <!-- Escape hatch for qe.gatlingKarate.javaRuntime (see the JDK 25/Karate
       Suite#run hang documented above): activates ONLY when that property
       is actually passed via -D, e.g.
       -Dqe.gatlingKarate.javaRuntime=/path/to/jdk21/bin/java. Left out of
       the main build/plugins block above because Surefire's <jvm> element
       rejects a bare "java" default (it must already be a real path), so
       there is no safe always-on default to give it. -->
  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <id>gatling-karate-jvm-override</id>
      <activation>
        <property>
          <name>qe.gatlingKarate.javaRuntime</name>
        </property>
      </activation>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <jvm>${qe.gatlingKarate.javaRuntime}</jvm>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
